// ---------- Saints library: deep profiles with museum art (NGA and the Met, Open Access, CC0) ----------
// img: k = file key in img/saints/, c = credit. life: chapters. words: their own words (sourced). myth: claims checked.
const SA_NGA="National Gallery of Art, Washington · CC0",SA_MET="The Metropolitan Museum of Art · CC0";
const SAINT_LIB=[
{id:"peter",n:"St Peter",t:"The fisherman who became the rock",d:"died c. AD 64–67",feast:"29 June (with St Paul) · Chair of Peter 22 February",pos:"center 20%",
 img:[{k:"peter",c:"Studio of Rubens, Saint Peter, c. 1616/1618 · "+SA_NGA}],
 tag:"Impulsive, loyal, cowardly at the worst moment, forgiven, and chosen anyway. The first pope was a work in progress.",
 facts:[["Born","Bethsaida, Galilee (John 1:44)"],["Work","Fisherman on the Sea of Galilee, with his brother Andrew"],["Died","Crucified in Rome under Nero"],["Buried","Vatican Hill, under today's St Peter's Basilica"],["Patron of","Popes, fishermen, the universal Church"],["Wrote","1 and 2 Peter; Mark's Gospel is traditionally his preaching"]],
 life:[
 {h:"Called from the boats",p:["Simon was a working fisherman from Capernaum, married (Jesus heals his mother-in-law in Mark 1:30), and the brother of Andrew, who brought him to Jesus. After a night of catching nothing, Jesus told him to let down the nets again; the catch nearly sank the boat. Simon fell at his knees: \"Depart from me, for I am a sinful man, O Lord\" (Luke 5:8). Jesus' answer was to call him."]},
 {h:"The rock",p:["At Caesarea Philippi Jesus asked who the disciples said he was. Simon answered, \"You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.\" Jesus renamed him Kephas, Aramaic for rock: \"On this rock I will build my Church… I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven\" (Matthew 16:16–19). In every list of the Twelve, Peter is named first."]},
 {h:"The denial and the second chance",p:["The man who swore he would die with Jesus denied knowing him three times before the cock crowed. After the resurrection, on the same lakeshore, beside a charcoal fire like the one he had warmed himself at during the denial, Jesus asked him three times, \"Do you love me?\" and three times gave him a task: \"Feed my sheep\" (John 21:15–17). Every failure was matched by a commission."]},
 {h:"Pentecost to Rome",p:["At Pentecost Peter preached and about three thousand were baptised (Acts 2). He healed in Jesus' name, was arrested, freed by an angel (Acts 12), baptised the first Gentile household, the Roman centurion Cornelius (Acts 10), and spoke decisively at the Council of Jerusalem (Acts 15). He ended in Rome, where he was crucified during Nero's persecution. Origen reports he asked to be crucified head downward, not thinking himself worthy to die as his Lord did."]},
 {h:"The bones under the altar",p:["Excavations beneath St Peter's in the 1940s found a first-century cemetery and a small shrine (the \"Trophy of Gaius,\" mentioned around AD 200) directly under the high altar. In 1968 Pope Paul VI announced that bones found in a nearby niche had been identified as Peter's \"in a way which we can hold to be convincing.\" Archaeologists still debate the details; the location of the tomb itself is widely accepted."]}],
 words:[{q:"Lord, to whom shall we go? You have the words of eternal life.",s:"John 6:68"},{q:"Cast all your anxieties on him, for he cares about you.",s:"1 Peter 5:7"}],
 myth:[{c:"\"Quo vadis?\" Peter fled Rome, met Jesus on the road and turned back.",v:"Legend. It comes from the apocryphal Acts of Peter (late 2nd century), not Scripture. A lovely story; not history."},{c:"Peter was never in Rome.",v:"Unlikely. Clement of Rome (c. 96), Ignatius (c. 107) and Irenaeus (c. 180) all connect Peter with Rome, and no ancient city ever competed for his tomb."}],
 why:"Peter is proof that God builds on people who fail. The Church's first leader is remembered as much for his worst night as for his best confession.",
 go:[["Read John 21 with the Fathers","#read-john-21-15"],["Matthew 16 with Aquinas","#read-matthew-16-13"]]},

{id:"paul",n:"St Paul",t:"The persecutor who became the apostle",d:"c. AD 5 – c. 64–67",feast:"29 June · Conversion 25 January",pos:"center 25%",
 img:[{k:"paul",c:"Rembrandt van Rijn (and Workshop?), The Apostle Paul, c. 1657 · "+SA_NGA},{k:"paul2",c:"Jacopo Tintoretto, The Conversion of Saint Paul, c. 1544 · "+SA_NGA}],
 tag:"He hunted Christians, then wrote almost half the New Testament. History's most dramatic career change.",
 facts:[["Born","Tarsus in Cilicia (south-east Turkey), a Roman citizen"],["Trained","As a Pharisee under Gamaliel in Jerusalem (Acts 22:3)"],["Trade","Tentmaker, which paid his way on mission"],["Wrote","13 New Testament letters bear his name"],["Died","Beheaded in Rome under Nero, as a citizen could not be crucified"],["Patron of","Missionaries, writers, journalists"]],
 life:[
 {h:"Saul the zealot",p:["Saul was a brilliant young Pharisee who watched the cloaks of the men who stoned Stephen, the first martyr (Acts 7:58), and then went house to house dragging Christians to prison. He was travelling to Damascus with letters authorising arrests when everything changed."]},
 {h:"The road to Damascus",p:["A light from heaven threw him to the ground and a voice asked, \"Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?\" Not \"my followers\": me. Christ identified himself with the Church. Saul was blinded for three days, baptised by Ananias, and began preaching the Jesus he had hunted (Acts 9). Notice the Christians' courage too: Ananias had to go and lay hands on the most feared man in the region."]},
 {h:"Three journeys and a shipwreck",p:["Over roughly fifteen years Paul planted churches across Asia Minor and Greece: Galatia, Philippi, Thessalonica, Corinth, Ephesus. His own list of hardships: five times given thirty-nine lashes, three times beaten with rods, once stoned, three times shipwrecked, a night and a day adrift at sea (2 Corinthians 11:24–27). He kept writing letters to the churches he had left, and those letters became Scripture."]},
 {h:"Rome",p:["Arrested in Jerusalem, he appealed to Caesar as a Roman citizen, survived a shipwreck on Malta (Acts 27–28), and spent two years under house arrest in Rome, still preaching. Tradition, recorded by Clement of Rome and Eusebius, says he was beheaded there under Nero. His tomb is at the Basilica of St Paul Outside the Walls; in 2009 Pope Benedict XVI announced that bone fragments in the sarcophagus had been carbon-dated to the first or second century."]}],
 words:[{q:"For to me to live is Christ, and to die is gain.",s:"Philippians 1:21"},{q:"I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith.",s:"2 Timothy 4:7"},{q:"Love is patient and kind; love is not jealous or boastful.",s:"1 Corinthians 13:4"}],
 myth:[{c:"Paul fell off a horse.",v:"Not in the text. Acts says he fell to the ground; the horse comes from centuries of paintings, including Tintoretto's here."},{c:"Paul invented Christianity and ignored Jesus.",v:"No. His letters are the earliest Christian writings we have, and they already worship Jesus as Lord (Philippians 2:6–11) and hand on the Last Supper and resurrection appearances he had received (1 Corinthians 11 and 15)."}],
 why:"Nobody is too far gone. The Church's greatest missionary was once its fiercest enemy.",
 go:[["Romans 8 with the Fathers","#read-romans-8-1"],["1 Corinthians 13","#read-1-corinthians-13-1"]]},

{id:"magdalene",n:"St Mary Magdalene",t:"Apostle to the apostles",d:"1st century",feast:"22 July",pos:"center 30%",
 img:[{k:"magdalene",c:"Artemisia Gentileschi, Mary Magdalene in Ecstasy, c. 1625 · "+SA_NGA}],
 tag:"The first person to see the risen Christ, and the most misunderstood woman in Church history.",
 facts:[["From","Magdala, a fishing town on the Sea of Galilee"],["Healed","Jesus cast seven demons out of her (Luke 8:2)"],["Witnessed","The crucifixion, the burial and the empty tomb"],["Title","\"Apostle to the apostles\", used in her feast-day preface since 2016"],["Patron of","Penitents, converts, perfumers"]],
 life:[
 {h:"Healed and following",p:["Luke tells us Jesus freed Mary of Magdala from seven demons, and that she was one of several women who travelled with him and supported his mission \"out of their means\" (Luke 8:1–3). That is almost all we know of her before Holy Week, and it is enough: a woman with money and independence who gave both to Jesus."]},
 {h:"At the cross",p:["When most of the apostles had run, Mary Magdalene stood near the cross with Jesus' mother and the beloved disciple (John 19:25). She watched where he was buried."]},
 {h:"The garden",p:["Early on Sunday she came to the tomb, found it empty and wept. A man she took for the gardener asked why she was crying. Then he said her name, \"Mary,\" and she knew him: \"Rabboni!\" Jesus sent her to tell the disciples, \"I have seen the Lord\" (John 20:11–18). The first announcement of the resurrection was entrusted to a woman, at a time when women's testimony carried little weight in court. That is one reason historians take the account seriously: nobody inventing a story would have chosen her."]}],
 words:[{q:"I have seen the Lord.",s:"John 20:18"}],
 myth:[{c:"She was a prostitute.",v:"Not in the Bible. The idea comes from a sermon by Pope Gregory the Great (591) that merged her with the sinful woman of Luke 7 and Mary of Bethany. The Church's calendar separated them again in 1969."},{c:"She married Jesus (The Da Vinci Code).",v:"No ancient evidence. The theory rests on late Gnostic texts, misread."},{c:"She ended her days in Provence.",v:"Medieval French legend. Lovely pilgrimage site; no early evidence."}],
 why:"She shows that love which stays when everyone else leaves gets to see what no one else sees.",
 go:[["John 20 with the Fathers","#read-john-20-1"]]},

{id:"augustine",n:"St Augustine of Hippo",t:"The African genius who said \"not yet\"",d:"354–430",feast:"28 August",pos:"center 20%",
 img:[{k:"augustine",c:"German 15th Century, Saint Augustine, 1450/1460, hand-coloured woodcut · "+SA_NGA},{k:"augustine2",c:"Joseph Hartmann, Saint Augustine Subdues Heresy with Lightning Bolts from His Pen, 1770s · "+SA_NGA}],
 tag:"A brilliant, restless, ambitious North African who tried everything, then became the most influential theologian in the West.",
 facts:[["Born","Thagaste, Roman North Africa (Souk Ahras, Algeria)"],["Parents","Monica, a Christian; Patricius, a pagan who converted before he died"],["Career","Professor of rhetoric in Carthage, Rome and Milan"],["Bishop","Of Hippo Regius (Annaba, Algeria) from about 395"],["Wrote","Confessions, City of God, On the Trinity; over 5 million words survive"],["Title","Doctor of the Church, \"Doctor of Grace\""]],
 life:[
 {h:"The restless student",p:["Augustine was gifted and knew it. At Carthage he lived with a woman for about fifteen years (they had a son, Adeodatus, \"given by God\"), joined the Manichees for nine years, and climbed the career ladder of rhetoric. His mother Monica prayed and wept for him for years. A bishop told her, \"It is not possible that the son of so many tears should perish.\""]},
 {h:"Milan and the garden",p:["In 384 he took a prestigious post in Milan, went to hear Bishop Ambrose preach for his style, and found himself taken by the content. In a garden in 386, torn between the life he wanted and the life he knew he should live, he heard a child's voice chanting \"tolle lege\", take up and read. He opened Paul's letters at Romans 13:13–14 and the struggle ended. Ambrose baptised him at Easter 387, together with Adeodatus. Monica died a few months later at Ostia, content."]},
 {h:"A bishop against his will",p:["Back in Africa he wanted a quiet monastic life. Visiting Hippo in 391, he was seized by the congregation and ordained a priest on the spot, weeping. Within a few years he was bishop, and remained so for thirty-five years: preaching several times a week, judging disputes, freeing slaves, and writing constantly against the Donatists and Pelagians."]},
 {h:"Confessions and City of God",p:["Around 397 he wrote the Confessions, the first great autobiography in Western literature, addressed to God. After Rome was sacked in 410 and pagans blamed the Christians, he spent thirteen years on The City of God: two cities, built by two loves, \"love of self even to contempt of God\" and \"love of God even to contempt of self.\" He died on 28 August 430 as the Vandals besieged Hippo, reading the penitential psalms he had pinned to his wall."]}],
 words:[{q:"You have made us for yourself, O Lord, and our heart is restless until it rests in you.",s:"Confessions I.1"},{q:"Late have I loved you, beauty so ancient and so new, late have I loved you!",s:"Confessions X.27"},{q:"Give me chastity and self-control, but not yet.",s:"Confessions VIII.7"}],
 myth:[{c:"Augustine was European.",v:"He was a Roman North African, born and buried in what is now Algeria, and the Church's greatest African theologian."},{c:"\"Love, and do what you will\" means anything goes.",v:"Badly misread. In his sermon on 1 John 4:4–12 he means: if your root is real love of God and neighbour, your actions will be good, even when they look severe."}],
 why:"For anyone who has run from God, wasted years, or kept saying \"not yet\": Augustine is your patron. The restlessness is a signpost.",
 go:[["Read the Confessions' favourite verses in Golden Chain","#read-romans-13-11"]]},

{id:"jerome",n:"St Jerome",t:"The grumpy genius who gave us the Bible",d:"c. 347–420",feast:"30 September",pos:"center 30%",
 img:[{k:"jerome",c:"Cima da Conegliano, Saint Jerome in the Wilderness, c. 1500/1505 · "+SA_NGA},{k:"jerome2",c:"Simon Vouet, Saint Jerome and the Angel, c. 1622/1625 · "+SA_NGA}],
 tag:"Brilliant, prickly, devoted to Scripture. He translated the Bible into the Latin the West read for over a thousand years.",
 facts:[["Born","Stridon, on the border of Dalmatia and Pannonia"],["Languages","Latin, Greek, and Hebrew learned as an adult in the desert"],["Major work","The Vulgate, the Latin Bible"],["Lived","Rome, the Syrian desert, Antioch, then 34 years in Bethlehem"],["Title","Doctor of the Church"],["Patron of","Translators, librarians, Scripture scholars"]],
 life:[
 {h:"Cicero or Christ?",p:["Jerome studied the classics in Rome and loved them. In a famous letter he describes a feverish dream in which he stood before the judgment seat and was asked who he was. \"A Christian,\" he said. \"You lie,\" came the reply, \"you are a Ciceronian, not a Christian\" (Letter 22). He went into the Syrian desert as a hermit, and to tame his mind he learned Hebrew from a Jewish convert, which he found harsh after elegant Latin."]},
 {h:"Secretary to a pope",p:["In 382 Pope Damasus made him his secretary and asked him to revise the Latin Gospels, which existed in many inconsistent versions. Jerome also gathered a circle of educated Roman women, including Paula and her daughter Eustochium, who studied Hebrew and Scripture with him. When Damasus died, Jerome's sharp tongue had made too many enemies and he left Rome."]},
 {h:"Bethlehem",p:["From 386 he lived in Bethlehem near the cave of the Nativity, where Paula founded monasteries. There he translated most of the Old Testament directly from the Hebrew instead of from the Greek Septuagint, a bold choice that even Augustine questioned. The result, the Vulgate, became the Bible of the Western Church; the Council of Trent declared it authentic in 1546."]},
 {h:"The temper",p:["Jerome feuded with his old friend Rufinus, wrote savage pamphlets against opponents, and could be unfair. He is a saint not because he was easy to like, but because he poured a difficult personality into the service of God's word. He died in Bethlehem in 420."]}],
 words:[{q:"Ignorance of the Scriptures is ignorance of Christ.",s:"Commentary on Isaiah, Prologue"},{q:"Love the Holy Scriptures, and wisdom will love you.",s:"Letter 130"}],
 myth:[{c:"Jerome pulled a thorn from a lion's paw and it lived with him.",v:"Legend, probably borrowed from St Gerasimus, a desert monk whose name was confused with Jerome's (Geronimus) in the Middle Ages. That's why painters always give him a lion."},{c:"Pope Sixtus V: \"You do well to beat your breast with that stone; without it the Church would never have made you a saint.\"",v:"A popular anecdote about a painting of Jerome; attributed, not documented. But it captures him."}],
 why:"If you have a difficult personality, Jerome is proof that sanctity is not niceness. Put your gifts and your temper at God's service, and read the Bible daily.",
 go:[["Read the Bible with the Fathers","#reader"]]},

{id:"benedict",n:"St Benedict of Nursia",t:"The father of Western monasticism",d:"c. 480 – c. 547",feast:"11 July",pos:"center 40%",
 img:[{k:"benedict",c:"Fra Filippo Lippi, Saint Benedict Orders Saint Maurus to the Rescue of Saint Placidus, c. 1445/1450 · "+SA_NGA}],
 tag:"He fled a decadent Rome, lived in a cave, and wrote a short rule that quietly rebuilt European civilisation.",
 facts:[["Born","Nursia (Norcia), Umbria, Italy"],["Twin sister","St Scholastica"],["Founded","Monte Cassino, c. 529"],["Wrote","The Rule of St Benedict: a prologue and 73 short chapters"],["Biography","Book II of the Dialogues of Pope St Gregory the Great (c. 593)"],["Patron of","Europe (named by Paul VI in 1964), students, against poison"]],
 life:[
 {h:"Leaving Rome",p:["Sent to study in Rome as Roman civilisation was collapsing, Benedict was repelled by the dissolute life of his fellow students and left. He lived as a hermit in a cave at Subiaco for three years; a monk named Romanus lowered bread to him on a rope."]},
 {h:"The poisoned cup",p:["His reputation spread and a nearby community of monks asked him to be their abbot. His discipline was too much for them and they poisoned his wine. When he made the sign of the cross over the cup, says Gregory, it shattered. He left them and founded twelve small monasteries of his own around Subiaco."]},
 {h:"Monte Cassino and the Rule",p:["Around 529 he moved to Monte Cassino, between Rome and Naples, and wrote his Rule. It begins with the word \"Listen.\" It balances prayer, work and reading, insists on hospitality (\"All guests who arrive should be received as Christ\"), and is famously moderate: enough food, enough sleep, a little wine. Benedictine monasteries copied manuscripts, farmed, ran schools and hospitals; much of ancient learning survived through them."]},
 {h:"Scholastica's storm",p:["Gregory tells how Benedict's twin sister Scholastica, also a nun, visited him once a year. On their last visit she begged him to stay and talk through the night. He refused; she prayed, and a storm broke so violently he could not leave. \"What have you done?\" \"I asked you and you would not listen; I asked my God and he listened.\" She died three days later. Benedict died around 547, standing in the chapel, supported by his monks."]}],
 words:[{q:"Listen carefully, my son, to the master's instructions, and attend to them with the ear of your heart.",s:"Rule, Prologue"},{q:"Idleness is the enemy of the soul.",s:"Rule, chapter 48"},{q:"Let all guests who arrive be received like Christ.",s:"Rule, chapter 53"}],
 myth:[{c:"\"Ora et labora\" (pray and work) is in the Rule.",v:"The phrase is a later summary, not in the text. It is a fair summary, though."}],
 why:"In a distracted age, Benedict offers a rhythm: pray, work, read, rest, welcome people. Small habits that rebuilt a continent.",
 go:[["Pray the Hours","#hours"]]},

{id:"francis",n:"St Francis of Assisi",t:"The rich kid who married Lady Poverty",d:"1181/82–1226",feast:"4 October",pos:"center 45%",
 img:[{k:"francis",c:"Domenico Veneziano, Saint Francis Receiving the Stigmata, c. 1445/1450 · "+SA_NGA}],
 tag:"Party boy, failed knight, then the most Christ-like man of the Middle Ages. Probably the most loved saint in the world.",
 facts:[["Born","Assisi, son of the wealthy cloth merchant Pietro di Bernardone"],["Founded","The Friars Minor (Franciscans); with St Clare, the Poor Clares"],["First","Recorded stigmatic, La Verna, 1224"],["Never","Ordained a priest; he remained a deacon"],["Canonised","1228, less than two years after his death"],["Patron of","Italy, animals, ecology (1979)"]],
 life:[
 {h:"The party and the prison",p:["Francis was charming, generous and fond of fine clothes and feasts. He went to war against Perugia, was captured and spent about a year in prison, then fell seriously ill. Setting out again for glory as a knight, he turned back after a dream. Something had shifted."]},
 {h:"The leper and the crucifix",p:["He forced himself to embrace a leper, which he had always found repulsive. Later he wrote: \"What had seemed bitter to me was turned into sweetness of soul and body.\" Praying in the crumbling church of San Damiano, he heard the crucifix say, \"Francis, go and repair my house, which is falling into ruin.\" He took it literally and began rebuilding churches with his own hands."]},
 {h:"Naked before the bishop",p:["To pay for the repairs he sold his father's cloth. His furious father hauled him before the bishop of Assisi. Francis stripped off his clothes, handed them back, and said he now had only one father, the one in heaven. The bishop covered him with his cloak. Companions joined him; in 1209 or 1210 Pope Innocent III approved their simple rule of living the Gospel in poverty."]},
 {h:"The sultan, the crib and the wounds",p:["In 1219, during the Fifth Crusade, he crossed the battle lines in Egypt to preach to Sultan al-Kamil, who received him courteously. At Greccio in 1223 he staged the first living Nativity scene. In September 1224, praying on Mount La Verna, he received the stigmata, the wounds of Christ in his hands, feet and side. Nearly blind and in pain, he composed the Canticle of the Creatures (\"Brother Sun, Sister Moon\") and died on 3 October 1226, lying on the bare ground."]}],
 words:[{q:"Praised be you, my Lord, through Brother Sun, who brings the day.",s:"Canticle of the Creatures"},{q:"What had seemed bitter to me was turned into sweetness of soul and body.",s:"Testament"}],
 myth:[{c:"\"Preach the Gospel at all times; use words if necessary.\"",v:"Not found in anything Francis wrote. He actually preached with words, constantly. His Rule does say friars should preach by their deeds."},{c:"The Peace Prayer (\"Make me an instrument of your peace\") is by Francis.",v:"No. It first appeared in a French magazine in 1912. Beautiful, Franciscan in spirit, not his."}],
 why:"Francis shows that joy and poverty are not opposites. Owning less can mean loving more.",
 go:[["The Sermon on the Mount with the Fathers","#read-matthew-5-1"]]},

{id:"dominic",n:"St Dominic",t:"The preacher who sold his books",d:"c. 1170–1221",feast:"8 August",pos:"center 40%",
 img:[{k:"dominic",c:"Cherubino Alberti, The Virgin of the Rosary with Saint Dominic and a Monk, c. 1570, engraving · "+SA_NGA}],
 tag:"Founder of the Order of Preachers, Aquinas' order. He believed you win hearts with truth, study and a holy life.",
 facts:[["Born","Caleruega, Castile, Spain"],["Founded","The Order of Preachers (Dominicans), approved 1216"],["Famous sons","Thomas Aquinas, Albert the Great, Fra Angelico, Catherine of Siena (lay Dominican)"],["Died","Bologna, 6 August 1221"],["Canonised","1234"],["Patron of","Astronomers, the falsely accused"]],
 life:[
 {h:"Books or people?",p:["As a student at Palencia during a famine, Dominic sold his precious annotated books to feed the poor. His biographer Jordan of Saxony records his reason: \"I will not study on dead skins while men are dying of hunger.\""]},
 {h:"The Cathar crisis",p:["Travelling through southern France with his bishop, Dominic met the Cathars, who taught that the material world was evil, and saw that papal legates riding in splendour convinced no one. He proposed the opposite: preachers who were poor, learned and holy, who would argue patiently and live what they preached. He reportedly stayed up a whole night in an inn debating with a Cathar innkeeper, who converted by morning."]},
 {h:"A new kind of order",p:["Pope Honorius III approved the Order of Preachers in 1216. Unlike monks bound to one monastery, Dominic's friars were mobile and sent straight to the great universities, Paris and Bologna, because preaching required study. That decision is why, a generation later, a young Thomas Aquinas ran away from his family to join them."]},
 {h:"Cheerful by day",p:["Witnesses at his canonisation remembered that he was always cheerful with people and spent nights weeping in prayer for sinners. He died in Bologna in 1221, leaving his friars, as he said, \"charity, humility and voluntary poverty.\""]}],
 words:[{q:"I will not study on dead skins while men are dying of hunger.",s:"Recorded by Jordan of Saxony, Libellus"},{q:"Arm yourself with prayer rather than a sword; wear humility rather than fine clothes.",s:"Attributed, early Dominican tradition"}],
 myth:[{c:"Mary gave Dominic the Rosary.",v:"A beautiful tradition, but historians trace it to Alan de la Roche in the 15th century, 250 years later. Dominicans did spread the Rosary enormously."}],
 why:"Dominic shows that study can be a work of love. Learning the faith properly is how you serve people who are searching.",
 go:[["Theology: disputed questions","#theology"]]},

{id:"aquinas",n:"St Thomas Aquinas",t:"The Dumb Ox who bellowed",d:"c. 1225–1274",feast:"28 January",pos:"center 20%",
 img:[{k:"zurbaran",c:"Francisco de Zurbarán, The Apotheosis of St Thomas Aquinas (detail), 1631 · Museo de Bellas Artes, Seville · public domain"},{k:"aquinas",c:"Italian 15th Century, Saint Thomas Aquinas, c. 1450, hand-coloured woodcut · "+SA_NGA},{k:"aquinas2",c:"Johann Heinrich Schönfeld, A Crucifix in a Church with Saints Thomas Aquinas, Mary Magdalene, Albertus Magnus and Others, 1655 · "+SA_NGA}],
 tag:"Kidnapped by his family, mocked by his classmates, then the greatest theologian of the Church. This whole app is named after his Golden Chain.",
 facts:[["Born","Roccasecca castle, near Aquino, Kingdom of Sicily"],["Order","Dominican, from 1244"],["Teacher","St Albert the Great, in Paris and Cologne"],["Wrote","Summa Theologiae, Summa contra Gentiles, Catena Aurea; over 8 million words"],["Titles","Angelic Doctor, Common Doctor; Doctor of the Church (1567)"],["Patron of","Students, universities, Catholic schools"]],
 life:[
 {h:"The family wanted an abbot",p:["The youngest son of a noble family, Thomas was given to the abbey of Monte Cassino at about five; his parents hoped he would become its abbot. At the University of Naples he met the new, poor, preaching Dominicans and joined them at about 19. His family was horrified. His brothers kidnapped him on the road and held him at the family castle for about a year."]},
 {h:"The firebrand",p:["According to his early biographers, the brothers sent a woman into his room to tempt him. Thomas seized a burning stick from the fire and drove her out, then traced a cross on the wall. That night, they recorded, two angels girded him with a cord of chastity. His mother eventually let him escape through a window, and he rejoined the Dominicans."]},
 {h:"The Dumb Ox",p:["At Cologne and Paris he studied under Albert the Great. Large, quiet and humble, he was nicknamed \"the Dumb Ox\" by classmates; one even offered to explain the lessons to him. When Albert heard him argue, he said: \"We call him the Dumb Ox, but one day his bellowing will be heard throughout the world.\""]},
 {h:"The Golden Chain and the Summa",p:["As a Master at Paris and in Italy he taught, preached and wrote at extraordinary speed, reportedly dictating to several secretaries at once. At the request of Pope Urban IV he compiled the Catena Aurea (begun 1263): the four Gospels explained verse by verse in the words of over 80 Church Fathers. He wrote the hymns for Corpus Christi, including Pange Lingua and Tantum Ergo. From about 1265 he wrote the Summa Theologiae, a complete map of theology in thousands of articles, each beginning with the strongest objections to his own view."]},
 {h:"Straw",p:["On 6 December 1273, while celebrating Mass in Naples, something happened to him. He stopped writing. When his secretary Reginald begged him to finish the Summa he said: \"I cannot, because all that I have written seems like straw compared to what I have seen.\" He died three months later, on 7 March 1274, at the abbey of Fossanova, on his way to the Council of Lyon. He was canonised in 1323."]}],
 words:[{q:"Grace does not destroy nature but perfects it.",s:"Summa Theologiae I, q.1, a.8, ad 2"},{q:"All that I have written seems like straw compared to what I have seen.",s:"Reported by Reginald of Piperno"},{q:"I adore you devoutly, hidden God.",s:"Adoro te devote (traditionally his)"}],
 myth:[{c:"\"To one who has faith, no explanation is necessary; to one without faith, no explanation is possible.\"",v:"Constantly attributed to Aquinas, not found in his works. It is also the opposite of his method: he spent his life explaining."},{c:"Aquinas proved God exists in five lines.",v:"The Five Ways (ST I q.2 a.3) are a summary of arguments he develops at length elsewhere, especially in the Summa contra Gentiles. They are compressed on purpose."}],
 why:"Thomas shows that faith and reason are allies. Ask the hardest objections, answer them honestly, and end on your knees.",
 go:[["Ask Aquinas: disputed questions","#theology"],["Read the Gospels with his Golden Chain","#reader"]]},

{id:"catherine",n:"St Catherine of Siena",t:"The young woman who told a pope to come home",d:"1347–1380",feast:"29 April",pos:"center 30%",
 img:[{k:"catherine",c:"Luigi Garzi, Saint Catherine of Siena on a Cloud, 1696/1697 · "+SA_NGA}],
 tag:"The 24th child of a dyer, with no formal schooling, who advised popes and became a Doctor of the Church. She died at 33.",
 facts:[["Born","Siena, 25 March 1347, the 24th of 25 children"],["Way of life","Lay Dominican (Mantellate), living at home"],["Wrote","The Dialogue; about 380 letters; prayers (dictated)"],["Titles","Doctor of the Church (1970); co-patron of Italy and of Europe"],["Died","Rome, 29 April 1380, aged 33"]],
 life:[
 {h:"A stubborn girl",p:["At six she had a vision of Christ above a church in Siena; at seven she privately vowed her virginity to God. When her parents pushed her toward marriage she cut off her hair. She won: they gave her a small room where she lived in silence and prayer for three years, then joined the Dominican laywomen of Siena."]},
 {h:"Into the plague",p:["Catherine then turned outward. She nursed the sick through the plague of 1374, cared for prisoners, and famously accompanied a young man condemned to death, Niccolò di Toldo, to the scaffold, holding his head as he died. A circle of disciples, priests and laymen, gathered around her and called her \"Mamma.\""]},
 {h:"Writing to a pope",p:["The popes had lived in Avignon, France, for nearly seventy years. Catherine wrote to Pope Gregory XI with astonishing frankness, calling him \"sweet Christ on earth\" and urging him to be a man and return to Rome. She went to Avignon in 1376. In January 1377 Gregory returned. Historians debate how much was her doing; contemporaries had no doubt she mattered."]},
 {h:"The Dialogue and the end",p:["In ecstatic prayer she dictated The Dialogue, a conversation between the soul and God about truth, love and the Church. When the Great Schism split the Church in 1378, she backed Pope Urban VI and moved to Rome to work for unity, exhausting herself. She died there in 1380 at 33. In 1970 Paul VI made her one of the first two women Doctors of the Church, with Teresa of Ávila."]}],
 words:[{q:"If you are what you should be, you will set fire to all Italy.",s:"Letter 368, to Stefano Maconi"},{q:"Nothing great is ever achieved without much enduring.",s:"Attributed, from her letters' recurring theme"}],
 myth:[{c:"\"Be who God meant you to be and you will set the world on fire.\"",v:"A popular paraphrase of Letter 368, which actually says \"all Italy.\" The meaning is faithful."}],
 why:"Catherine is proof that age, gender and schooling are no barrier to changing the Church. She spoke truth to the powerful because she loved them.",
 go:[["Prayers for every part of your day","#prayers"]]},

{id:"joan",n:"St Joan of Arc",t:"The teenager who led an army",d:"c. 1412–1431",feast:"30 May",pos:"center 50%",
 img:[{k:"joan",c:"Louis Maurice Boutet de Monvel, The Vision and Inspiration (Joan of Arc series: I), c. 1907–1909 · "+SA_NGA},{k:"joan2",c:"Boutet de Monvel, The Maid in Armor on Horseback (III), c. 1908–1909 · "+SA_NGA},{k:"joan3",c:"Boutet de Monvel, The Trial of Joan of Arc (VI), c. 1909–1910 · "+SA_NGA}],
 tag:"An illiterate farm girl who heard saints, saved a kingdom, outwitted her judges, and was burned at 19.",
 facts:[["Born","Domrémy, eastern France, c. 1412"],["Voices","St Michael, St Catherine and St Margaret, from about age 13"],["Victory","Lifted the siege of Orléans in nine days, May 1429"],["Died","Burned at the stake in Rouen, 30 May 1431, aged about 19"],["Verdict","Overturned by a papal retrial in 1456"],["Canonised","1920; patron of France and of soldiers"]],
 life:[
 {h:"The voices",p:["France was losing the Hundred Years' War and the uncrowned Dauphin Charles controlled only part of the country. In a village garden, Joan began hearing voices she identified as St Michael, St Catherine and St Margaret. They told her to go to the Dauphin and drive the English out."]},
 {h:"Orléans",p:["At 17 she convinced a local captain to send her to Charles at Chinon. Theologians examined her at Poitiers and found nothing against her. Given armour and a banner, she rode with the army to the besieged city of Orléans and, in nine days in May 1429, the siege was broken. Wounded by an arrow, she returned to the fight. In July, Charles was crowned at Reims with Joan beside him."]},
 {h:"The trial",p:["Captured at Compiègne in 1430, she was sold to the English and tried for heresy at Rouen by a court led by the pro-English Bishop Pierre Cauchon. The judges tried to trap her with the question, \"Are you in God's grace?\" Yes would be presumptuous; no would be a confession. The 19-year-old answered: \"If I am not, may God put me there; and if I am, may God so keep me.\" The notary recorded that her questioners were stunned."]},
 {h:"The fire and the retrial",p:["She was burned on 30 May 1431, asking for a cross to be held before her eyes and calling on the name of Jesus. In 1456 a papal commission annulled the verdict, declaring the trial fraudulent. She was canonised in 1920."]}],
 words:[{q:"If I am not, may God put me there; and if I am, may God so keep me.",s:"Trial record, 24 February 1431"},{q:"I am not afraid; I was born to do this.",s:"Attributed"}],
 myth:[{c:"The Church burned Joan of Arc.",v:"A political church court under English control did; the Pope's own commission declared it unjust in 1456, and the Church later canonised her."}],
 why:"Joan shows what happens when someone very young says a total yes to God. Courage is not the absence of fear; it is obedience in spite of it.",
 go:[["Pray before you're brave","#prayers"]]},

{id:"more",n:"St Thomas More",t:"The king's good servant, but God's first",d:"1478–1535",feast:"22 June (with St John Fisher)",pos:"center 30%",
 img:[{k:"more",c:"Willem and Magdalena de Passe, Sir Thomas More, published 1620, engraving after Holbein · "+SA_NGA}],
 tag:"Lawyer, author, father, wit, Lord Chancellor of England. He lost everything rather than sign a lie.",
 facts:[["Born","London, 7 February 1478"],["Career","Lawyer, member of Parliament, Lord Chancellor (1529–1532)"],["Wrote","Utopia (1516), from which we get the word"],["Family","Educated his daughters as fully as his son, unusual then"],["Died","Beheaded on Tower Hill, 6 July 1535"],["Patron of","Statesmen and politicians (2000), lawyers"]],
 life:[
 {h:"Lawyer and family man",p:["More nearly became a Carthusian monk and lived beside the London Charterhouse for four years, but discerned he was called to marriage and public life. He married Jane Colt and had four children; after her death he married Alice Middleton. His house in Chelsea became famous for learning and laughter. His daughter Margaret Roper was one of the most educated women in England."]},
 {h:"The king's friend",p:["Henry VIII loved More's company and made him Lord Chancellor in 1529, the first layman in that office for generations. Underneath his robes he wore a hair shirt; at home he prayed the Hours and sometimes sang in the parish choir."]},
 {h:"The divorce",p:["When Henry sought to annul his marriage to Catherine of Aragon and declared himself Supreme Head of the Church in England, More resigned in 1532 and kept silent. Silence was not enough. In 1534 he refused to swear the oath attached to the Act of Succession, and spent fifteen months in the Tower of London."]},
 {h:"The scaffold",p:["He was convicted of treason on the perjured testimony of Richard Rich. He kept his humour to the end. Climbing the shaky scaffold he said, \"See me safe up; for my coming down, let me shift for myself.\" His last words, reported in contemporary accounts, were that he died \"the king's good servant, but God's first.\" He was canonised with Bishop John Fisher, who died for the same cause, in 1935."]}],
 words:[{q:"I die the king's good servant, but God's first.",s:"Reported at his execution, 6 July 1535"},{q:"Give me the grace, good Lord, to set the world at nought.",s:"Prayer written in the Tower"}],
 myth:[{c:"More was a gentle martyr with no flaws.",v:"As Lord Chancellor he pursued Protestant writers harshly and supported the burning of heretics, as the law then required. He is a saint for his fidelity and courage, not because every act was right."}],
 why:"More shows that you can be brilliant, funny, ambitious, married and in public life, and still put conscience first when it costs everything.",
 go:[["Conscience in the Catechism","#catechism"]]},

{id:"ignatius",n:"St Ignatius of Loyola",t:"The soldier whose leg changed history",d:"1491–1556",feast:"31 July",pos:"center 20%",
 img:[{k:"ignatius",c:"Schelte Adams Bolswert after Peter Paul Rubens, Saint Ignatius de Loyola, 1622 · "+SA_NGA}],
 tag:"A vain soldier hit by a cannonball, bored in bed with nothing to read but saints' lives. He founded the Jesuits.",
 facts:[["Born","Loyola, Basque Country, Spain, 1491, as Íñigo"],["Wounded","Pamplona, 20 May 1521, by a cannonball"],["Wrote","The Spiritual Exercises"],["Founded","The Society of Jesus (Jesuits), approved 1540"],["Companions","St Francis Xavier, St Peter Faber"],["Canonised","1622, with Teresa of Ávila and Francis Xavier"]],
 life:[
 {h:"Vanity and a cannonball",p:["Íñigo was a courtier and soldier who, in his own words, delighted in the exercise of arms \"with a great and vain desire to win fame.\" Defending Pamplona against the French in 1521, his leg was shattered by a cannonball. He had it rebroken and reset, without anaesthetic, so that it would look good in tight courtly hose."]},
 {h:"Two kinds of daydream",p:["Recovering at Loyola, he asked for romances; the house had only a life of Christ and a book of saints' lives. He noticed something: daydreams of knightly glory delighted him at the time but left him dry and sad; imagining doing what the saints did left him peaceful and joyful long afterwards. This observation became the core of his discernment of spirits: notice where consolation and desolation come from."]},
 {h:"Manresa to Paris",p:["He hung up his sword before the Black Madonna of Montserrat and spent most of a year in prayer at Manresa, where he drafted the Spiritual Exercises. He went back to school at 33, sitting with boys to learn Latin, was investigated by the Inquisition more than once, and finally studied in Paris, where he gathered companions including Francis Xavier and Peter Faber. On 15 August 1534 seven of them made vows together in Montmartre."]},
 {h:"The Jesuits",p:["Pope Paul III approved the Society of Jesus in 1540. Within decades Jesuits had founded hundreds of schools and colleges, sent missionaries to India, Japan, China and the Americas, and become central to the Catholic renewal. Ignatius ran it from a small room in Rome until his death in 1556."]}],
 words:[{q:"Take, Lord, and receive all my liberty, my memory, my understanding and my entire will.",s:"Suscipe, Spiritual Exercises §234"},{q:"For the greater glory of God (Ad maiorem Dei gloriam).",s:"Jesuit motto"}],
 myth:[{c:"\"Pray as if everything depends on God, work as if everything depends on you.\"",v:"A popular paraphrase. The original Jesuit maxim (recorded by Hevenesi, 1705) is subtler: trust God as if everything depended on you, yet work as if everything depended on God."}],
 why:"Ignatius teaches that God speaks through your desires and moods. Pay attention to what brings lasting peace, and choose that.",
 go:[["Try a daily examen in Prayers","#prayers"]]},

{id:"teresa",n:"St Teresa of Ávila",t:"The mystic with a sense of humour",d:"1515–1582",feast:"15 October",pos:"center 35%",
 img:[{k:"teresa",c:"Peter Paul Rubens, Saint Teresa of Ávila Interceding for Souls in Purgatory · "+SA_MET},{k:"teresa2",c:"Pierre van Schuppen, Saint Teresa of Ávila's Vision of the Dove, c. 1650 · "+SA_MET}],
 tag:"Ran away to be martyred at seven, was mediocre at prayer for twenty years, then reformed the Carmelites and wrote mystical classics.",
 facts:[["Born","Ávila, Spain, 28 March 1515"],["Reformed","The Carmelites: 17 convents of the Discalced (barefoot) Carmelites"],["Wrote","Life, The Way of Perfection, The Interior Castle"],["Friend","St John of the Cross"],["Titles","First woman Doctor of the Church (1970)"],["Died","4 October 1582, the night Spain switched calendars; the next day was 15 October"]],
 life:[
 {h:"Martyrdom at seven",p:["As a child Teresa read the lives of martyrs with her brother Rodrigo and decided the quickest way to heaven was to be beheaded by the Moors. They set off from Ávila and were brought back by an uncle. She was lively, charming and fond of novels and nice clothes as a teenager."]},
 {h:"Twenty dry years",p:["She entered the Carmelite convent of the Incarnation in 1535. A severe illness left her paralysed for some time. For nearly twenty years her prayer was half-hearted; she admits she watched the hourglass waiting for prayer to end. In 1554, before an image of the wounded Christ, she broke down, and her life of prayer truly began."]},
 {h:"The reform",p:["Teresa wanted small, poor, enclosed communities devoted to prayer. In 1562 she founded St Joseph's in Ávila, against fierce opposition, and spent the next twenty years travelling Spain by mule cart founding convents, negotiating with bishops, buying houses, and writing. With the young friar John of the Cross she extended the reform to men."]},
 {h:"The Interior Castle",p:["In 1577 she wrote The Interior Castle, describing the soul as a castle of crystal with seven sets of rooms (mansions), with God dwelling at the centre. Prayer is the door. She wrote with humour and plain speech, and her letters show a shrewd businesswoman. She died in 1582 at Alba de Tormes, saying, \"Lord, it is time to set out.\""]}],
 words:[{q:"Let nothing disturb you, let nothing frighten you. All things pass; God does not change. Patience obtains everything. Whoever has God lacks nothing. God alone suffices.",s:"Found in her breviary (bookmark)"},{q:"Mental prayer is nothing else than an intimate sharing between friends; it means taking time frequently to be alone with him who we know loves us.",s:"Life, chapter 8"}],
 myth:[{c:"\"Lord, if this is how you treat your friends, no wonder you have so few!\"",v:"Attributed after she fell off a cart into a river. It is a tradition, not documented in her writings. Entirely in character."},{c:"\"From silly devotions and sour-faced saints, good Lord, deliver us.\"",v:"Also attributed; widely quoted, uncertain source."}],
 why:"If prayer feels dry and you're tempted to quit: Teresa was mediocre for twenty years. Keep showing up. Prayer is friendship, not performance.",
 go:[["Learn to pray: Lectio Divina","#prayers"]]},

{id:"lucy",n:"St Lucy",t:"The light in December",d:"died 304",feast:"13 December",pos:"center 20%",
 img:[{k:"lucy",c:"Francisco de Zurbarán, Saint Lucy, c. 1625/1630 · "+SA_NGA}],
 tag:"A young Sicilian martyr whose name means light, honoured in the darkest days of the year.",
 facts:[["From","Syracuse, Sicily"],["Died","Martyred in the persecution of Diocletian, 304"],["Named in","The Roman Canon of the Mass, one of only seven women"],["Patron of","The blind, eye diseases, Syracuse"],["Name","From Latin lux, light"]],
 life:[
 {h:"What history tells us",p:["Lucy was certainly a real and early martyr: an inscription found in the catacombs of Syracuse, from around 400, already honours her feast, and her name was added to the Roman Canon of the Mass. She died in Diocletian's persecution, the last and fiercest of the Roman persecutions."]},
 {h:"The story told about her",p:["Her Acts, written a century or two later, tell how she persuaded her mother to give her dowry to the poor after her mother was healed at the tomb of St Agatha. Her rejected suitor denounced her as a Christian. The governor ordered her taken to a brothel, but she could not be moved, not even by a team of oxen. She was killed by a sword to the throat."]},
 {h:"The eyes",p:["Later legend said her eyes were put out, or that she tore them out to discourage a suitor and God restored them. That is why Zurbarán paints her holding her eyes on a dish. It is iconography, not history; but it fits a saint called \"light.\""]},
 {h:"Light in the darkness",p:["Under the old Julian calendar her feast fell near the winter solstice, the longest night of the year. In Scandinavia, Santa Lucia processions still have girls in white with crowns of candles singing in the dark on 13 December. In the Southern Hemisphere it's summer, but Advent is still a season of light coming into darkness."]}],
 words:[{q:"Those who live chastely and devoutly are temples of the Holy Spirit.",s:"Attributed to her in her Acts"}],
 myth:[{c:"Lucy tore out her own eyes.",v:"Medieval legend, not in the earliest accounts."}],
 why:"Lucy is a reminder that even in the darkest season, light wins. A young woman's faith outlasted an empire's last great persecution.",
 go:[["Advent with the Fathers","#mass"]]},

{id:"nicholas",n:"St Nicholas of Myra",t:"The real Santa Claus",d:"4th century",feast:"6 December",pos:"center 50%",
 img:[{k:"nicholas",c:"Gentile da Fabriano, The Crippled and Sick Cured at the Tomb of Saint Nicholas, 1425 · "+SA_NGA}],
 tag:"A bishop known for secret generosity, whose story travelled from Turkey to Italy to the Netherlands and became Santa Claus.",
 facts:[["Bishop of","Myra, in Lycia (south-west Turkey)"],["Lived","4th century; traditionally died 6 December, around 343"],["Relics","Taken to Bari, Italy, by sailors in 1087"],["Patron of","Children, sailors, the falsely accused, Russia"],["Name became","Sinterklaas (Dutch), then Santa Claus"]],
 life:[
 {h:"What we know",p:["Very little about Nicholas is documented in his own lifetime, but devotion to him is ancient and enormous: churches were dedicated to him across the Greek and Latin worlds by the 6th century. He was bishop of Myra during or just after the Great Persecution."]},
 {h:"Gold through the window",p:["The most famous story: a poor man had three daughters but no money for dowries, which meant they could not marry and might be forced into prostitution. On three nights Nicholas secretly threw a bag of gold through the window. On the third night the father caught him; Nicholas begged him to tell no one. This is why he is painted with three golden balls, and why secret gifts are his signature."]},
 {h:"Saving the innocent",p:["Early accounts (from the 6th century on) say he intervened to stop the execution of three innocent men, seizing the executioner's sword, and that he appeared in a dream to the emperor Constantine to save three falsely accused generals. Hence his patronage of the falsely accused."]},
 {h:"From Myra to Bari to your Christmas tree",p:["In 1087 sailors from Bari carried his relics from Myra to Italy, where they rest today; a clear liquid called \"manna\" is still collected from the tomb each year. Dutch devotion to Sinterklaas crossed to New Amsterdam (New York), and by the 19th century he had become Santa Claus."]}],
 words:[],
 myth:[{c:"Nicholas slapped (or punched) the heretic Arius at the Council of Nicaea.",v:"A late legend; early lists of Nicaea's bishops don't even all include him. Fun meme, not history."}],
 why:"Nicholas teaches the best kind of generosity: secret, practical, aimed at people in real danger.",
 go:[["Advent readings","#mass"]]}
];

;PACK_DONE("deep");
